1️⃣ Lazy execution: What gets logged?

    import { of, tap } from 'rxjs';

    const obs$ = of(1, 2, 3).pipe(
        tap(x => console.log('tap', x))
    );
    console.log('before');
    console.log('after');


    answer:     before  ✅
                after
    
    (🔥Because Observables in RxJS are lazy. Creating an Observable does nothing by itself unless we
    subscribe to it)



2️⃣ Subscription trigger: What gets logged?

    import { of, tap } from 'rxjs';

    const obs$ = of(1, 2, 3).pipe(
        tap(x => console.log('processing', x))
    );
    console.log('A');
    obs$.subscribe();
    console.log('B');


    answer:     A
                processing 1
                processing 2
                processing 3
                B



3️⃣ Cold observable: What gets logged?

    import { Observable } from 'rxjs';

    const obs$ = new Observable(subscriber => {
        console.log('Observable executed');
        subscriber.next(1);
    });
    obs$.subscribe(x => console.log('sub1', x));
    obs$.subscribe(x => console.log('sub2', x));


    answer:     Observable executed
                sub1 1
                Observable executed
                sub2 1
    
    (🔥A cold Observable runs its producer function once per subscription.)



4️⃣ If an observable users$ emits a UserModel, how do you get the emitted value and store it in a signal
of the component called user?

        users$.subscribe((event: Event<UserModel>) => this.user.set(event.data))

        users$.subscribe((user: UserModel) => this.user.set(user))  ✅

        this.user = toSignal(users$)    ✅

        this.user = toObservable(users$)

        this.user = users$.subscribe()



5️⃣ How do you switch a boolean signal called error when a potential error arises in the observable?

        users$.subscribe({ next: user => this.user.set(user), error: () => this.error.set(true) })  ✅

        try { users$.subscribe(user => this.user.set(user)) } catch (e) { this.error.set(true) }

        this.user = toSignal(users$.pipe(tap({ error: () => this.error.set(true) })))   ✅



6️⃣ How do you filter the users with an ID greater than 1000 emitted by an observable users$?

        users$.pipe(filter(user => user.id > 1000)) ✅

        users$.filter(user => user.id > 1000)   

        filter(users$, user => user.id > 1000)



7️⃣ How do you extract the ID of a user emitted by an observable users$?

        users$.pipe(map(user => user.id))   ✅

        users$.map(user => user.id)

        map(users$, user => user.id)



8️⃣ How do you extract the IDs of users with an ID greater than 1000 emitted by an observable users$?

        users$.pipe(map(user => user.id), filter(id => id > 1000))  ✅

        users$.map(user => user.id).filter(id => id > 1000)

        filter(map(users$, user => user.id), id => id > 1000)

        users$.pipe(filter(user => user.id > 1000), map(user => user.id)) ✅



